413e-06,-7.152555099310121e-07],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,8500,315,6785,25780,374,20346,553,14838,279,1156,220,19,11,1221,42659,825,11,14838,279,1790,220,20,11,42659,220,17,11,14838,220,21,11,42659,220,18,11,323,11,389,279,400,77,3,339,19569,11,14838,400,77,488,220,18,3,323,42659,400,77,12947,576,8500,12033,220,16,11,220,17,11,220,18,11,220,19,11,220,21,11,220,22,11,220,23,11,220,24,11,220,16,15,11,220,16,18,13,3555,374,279,220,20,15,15,11,15,15,15,339,1372,304,279,8500,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA sequence of positive integers is constructed by listing the first 4, then skipping one, listing the next 5, skipping 2, listing 6, skipping 3, and, on the $n$th iteration, listing $n + 3$ and skipping $n$. The sequence begins 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 13. What is the 500,000th number in the sequence?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"0c3069ba06ebec666a138574bce211fc11b931bd1f2000d20d7b7e08b9d014e9"},"seconds":23.401581048965454,"seed":610940988516179686,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to understand the pattern in which the sequence is generated. The sequence is constructed by listing a certain number of integers and then skipping a certain number of integers, with the number of integers listed and skipped increasing step by step.\n\nThe sequence is generated as follows:\n- On the 1st iteration, list 4 and skip 1.\n- On the 2nd iteration, list 5 and skip 2.\n- On the 3rd iteration, list 6 and skip 3.\n- On the \\( n \\)-th iteration, list \\( n + 3 \\) and skip \\( n \\).\n\nThe total number of integers listed and skipped up to the \\( n \\)-th iteration is:\n\\[ 4 + 5 + 6 + \\cdots + (n+3) + (1 + 2 + 3 + \\cdots + n) \\]\n\nThe sum of the first \\( n \\) positive integers is \\( \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} \\). So, the total number of integers listed and skipped up to the \\( n \\)-th iteration is:\n\\[ \\sum_{k=4}^{n+3} k + \\sum_{k=1}^{n} k = \\frac{(n+3)(n+4)}{2} - 6 + \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} \\]\n\\[ = \\frac{(n+3)(n+4) + n(n+1) - 12}{2} = \\frac{n^2 + 7n + 12 + n^2 + n - 12}{2} = \\frac{2n^2 + 8n}{2} = n^2 + 4n \\]\n\nWe need to find the largest \\( n \\) such that \\( n^2 + 4n \\leq 500,000 \\). Solving the quadratic inequality:\n\\[ n^2 + 4n - 500,000 \\leq 0 \\]\nUsing the quadratic formula \\( n = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 4 \\), and \\( c = -500,000 \\):\n\\[ n = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 2,000,000}}{2} = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{2,000,016}}{2} = \\frac{-4 \\pm 1414.23}{2} \\]\nTaking the positive root:\n\\[ n = \\frac{1410.23}{2} = 705.115 \\]\nSo, the largest integer \\( n \\) is 705. We check \\( n = 705 \\) and \\( n = 706 \\):\n\\[ 705^2 + 4 \\cdot 705 = 497425 \\]\n\\[ 706^2 + 4 \\cdot 706 = 501936 \\]\nSince \\( 497425 \\leq 500000 \\leq 501936 \\), the 500,000th number is in the 706th iteration.\n\nThe number of integers listed up to the 705th iteration is 497425. The 500,000th number is the 2555th number in the 706th iteration. The 706th iteration lists 709 numbers, so the 2555th number in this iteration is the 2555th number after the first 497425 numbers. The first number in the 706th iteration is the number after 497425, which is 497426. The 2555th number in this sequence is:\n\\[ 497426 + 2554 = 499980 \\]\n\nThus, the 500,000th number in the sequence is \\(\\boxed{499980}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{499980}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:661"}
